body:
Would it be equivalent to add this to the UA stylesheet?

```css
[popover]:not(:open):not(dialog[open]) {
  display: none;
}
```

I added a note based on the comment in chromium's HTMLElement::NearestAncestralPopover, and I removed the references to the anchor attribute.
@foolip did I put it in the right spot? Does it make sense?
@mfreed7 what do you think of it?

There's a behavior here that depends on the implementation details and I want to check it's intentional. As this is now written, together with https://github.com/w3c/pointerevents/pull/460, calling `event.preventDefault()` on the "pointerup" event won't prevent this step from running. Is that the intended behavior and is it tested by WPT?

FWIW, I think a more typical behavior would be if we run these steps only if the event wasn't canceled, and you'd get that by changing https://github.com/w3c/pointerevents/pull/460 to call this algorithm after event dispatch.

@domenic do you agree this is the right setup?

> To be clear, we're working toward WHATWG consensus here, not OpenUI consensus. For that we need to continue the above discussion.

We definitely agree that we need to reach consensus here in WHATWG! But hopefully the input and careful discourse among the developers in OpenUI should be treated as an information-rich input to this consensus process, right?

> But if you have a series of demos showing how two events would be more awkward than one, and that the above strange semantics don't cause problems, and that it's important for those demos that the event be sometimes sync and sometimes async (i.e., the demos would be worse if it was always async), then I'd be convinced.

So we have a number of demos that use popover, including all of the ones in the [blog post](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/pop-ups-theyre-making-a-resurgence/) (raw example code [here](https://codepen.io/collection/NqjRNE)), and the [`<selectmenu>` demo page](https://microsoftedge.github.io/Demos/selectmenu/) which uses popovers. None of these demos seem to be affected by the corner case we're discussing here, where an open popover is removed from the DOM. These aren't, of course, "real world" demos, but they are at least fairly exhaustive of the API functionality.


Based on this conversation, I [re-opened this issue](https://github.com/openui/open-ui/issues/578#issuecomment-1372671306) yesterday with OpenUI, and we discussed it again. The comments were similar to the last few discussions we had on this topic, with (I think) the conclusion being that as long as the developer can correctly deduce the situation within the event handler, the async event is ok.

I think it's important to note that **this is a corner case**: removing an element that is *currently* an open popover. Aside from vdom frameworks, that's likely never done. Clearly some framework code might need to special case this, as they already do for focus management, inputs, iframes, etc. So the corner case needs to be able to be handled, but it needn't be as ergonomic, especially if those corner case ergonomics make the ordinary use cases more difficult, right? And I'm guessing the events aren't even the problem for frameworks - they'll need to make sure never to yank an open popover out of the DOM in the first place, to avoid closing it.

Either way, the key question is whether, from an event handler, the situation can be determined. I think it can:

```javascript
addEventListener('popovertoggle',(e) => {
  const isHidingTransition = e.newState === 'closed';
  const isCurrentlyHidden = e.target.matches(':closed');
  const wasAsyncCornerCaseEvent = isHidingTransition && !isCurrentlyHidden;
  // If super-corner-case situations like multiple show/hide cycles need to be handled,
  // this can adjust by counting transitions, etc., so long as *all* events are always
  // fired, even for the corner cases.
}
```

and the normal use case event listener just looks like this:

```javascript
addEventListener('popovertoggle',(e) => {
  if (e.newState === 'closed') {
    // Do closing stuff
  } else {
    // Do opening stuff
  }
}
```

Sorry for the verbose answer, but I'm trying to convey the OpenUI sentiment, and the issue is nuanced.
